<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Transcaspian Government</h1><p class="st-meta">1918 CE – 1919 CE · capital Ashgabat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An anti-Bolshevik government of railway workers and Socialist Revolutionaries in Ashgabat, remembered for the execution of the 26 Baku Commissars.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In July 1918 railway workers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-revolutionary-party">Socialist Revolutionaries</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashgabat">Ashgabat</a> overthrew the local Soviet authorities of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turkestan-assr">Turkestan ASSR</a>, forming the Transcaspian Provisional Government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-funtikov">Fyodor Funtikov</a>. Facing Red forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a>, it appealed for help to the British in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The government controlled the Trans-Caspian railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenbasy">Krasnovodsk</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, relying on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> and Russian volunteers and a small British force under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid-malleson">Wilfrid Malleson</a>. In September 1918 its men shot the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/26-baku-commissars">26 Baku Commissars</a> in the desert east of Krasnovodsk, an event that became a major Soviet symbol of British intervention.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the British withdrew in 1919, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> advanced along the railway, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashgabat">Ashgabat</a> in July 1919 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenbasy">Krasnovodsk</a> in February 1920. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transcaspian-oblast">Transcaspia</a> returned to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turkestan-assr">Turkestan ASSR</a> and later became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turkmen-ssr">Turkmen SSR</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/transcaspian-government.jpg" alt="Transcaspian Government" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sports9494 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Russia_(1896%E2%80%931918).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1918 CE – 1919 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ashgabat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fyodor Funtikov</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian, Turkmen</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ashkhabad Committee, Transcaspian Provisional Government</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=transcaspian-government" aria-label="Transcaspian Government on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6135 813 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Transcaspian Government lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6455 1069L4997 651M6455 1069L6691 960"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M6691 960h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6455 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6455 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ashgabat. <a href="/atlas?year=1918&amp;focus=transcaspian-government">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

